.TH GETHOSTBYNAME_R 3
.SH NAME
gethostbyname_r, gethostbyname2_r, gethostbyaddr_r, gethostent_r \- reentrant network host database lookups
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library
.RI ( libc ", " \-lc )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <netdb.h>
.PP
.BI "int gethostbyname_r(const char *" name ", struct hostent *" ret ,
.BI "char *" buf ", size_t " buflen ", struct hostent **" result ", int *" h_errnop ");"
.br
.BI "int gethostbyname2_r(const char *" name ", int " af ", struct hostent *" ret ,
.BI "char *" buf ", size_t " buflen ", struct hostent **" result ", int *" h_errnop ");"
.br
.BI "int gethostbyaddr_r(const void *" addr ", socklen_t " len ", int " type ,
.BI "struct hostent *" ret ", char *" buf ", size_t " buflen ,
.BI "struct hostent **" result ", int *" h_errnop ");"
.br
.BI "int gethostent_r(struct hostent *" ret ", char *" buf ", size_t " buflen ,
.BI "struct hostent **" result ", int *" h_errnop ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
These are the reentrant equivalents of
.BR gethostbyname (3),
.BR gethostbyaddr (3)
and
.BR gethostent (3).
The result is built in the caller-supplied
.I ret
structure, with all the strings and the alias/address vectors copied into the
caller-supplied scratch buffer
.I buf
of length
.IR buflen .
On success
.I *result
is set to point at
.IR ret ;
when the host is not found
.I *result
is set to NULL.
.I *h_errnop
receives the
.BR h_errno (3)-style
status.
.PP
.B gethostbyname2_r
takes an address family
.IR af ;
substrate's resolver is IPv4-only, so only
.B AF_INET
yields results.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Return 0 on success (whether or not a host was found).  If
.I buf
is too small, ERANGE is returned and the caller should retry with a larger
buffer.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B ERANGE
.I buf
was too small to hold the result.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gethostbyname (3),
.BR getaddrinfo (3),
.BR getservbyname_r (3)
.SH STANDARDS
GNU extension.
